<!--
    Rec 39 (#39-4b, DD-0007): inlined popcount detection. The GCC/Clang software population-count
    expansion for a target without a hardware instruction is a fixed, loop-free dataflow shape over
    magic constants:
        x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55..);
        x = (x & 0x33..) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33..);
        x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f..;
        result = (x * 0x01..) >> (W - 8);
    RulePopcount anchors on the terminating multiply-then-shift, walks the four stages backwards,
    verifies every width-scaled magic constant, and on a complete match rewrites the root shift in
    place to the native POPCOUNT op (then sweeps the now-dead SWAR arithmetic). Unlike RuleMemset
    (#39-4a) it mints no builtin: Ghidra already ships CPUI_POPCOUNT / TypeOpPopcount, so the idiom
    folds into POPCOUNT(x).

    pc32   - 32-bit SWAR popcount, subtract stage-1 form: folds to a clean `return POPCOUNT(a);`,
             with the intermediate masking/shift arithmetic removed.
    pc64   - 64-bit SWAR popcount. gcc -O2 strength-reduces the 64-bit `imul 0x0101010101010101 >> 56`
             terminator into a truncated 32-bit byte-sum BEFORE the cleanup pool runs, so the canonical
             shape RulePopcount keys on no longer exists. This is the documented boundary of #39-4b:
             the rule must NOT misfire here, leaving the 64-bit stage-1 mask 0x5555555555555555 intact.
             (Recognising the reduced 64-bit terminator is deferred to a follow-up.)
    pcbad  - 32-bit near-miss; stage-1 mask is 0x55555554 (not 0x55555555). The match is exact, so
             RulePopcount must DECLINE and the raw arithmetic is preserved (no POPCOUNT).
-->
